rewritten_text:
Dawn had come when I set the Manuscript aside. The rain had almost stopped; the world was gray and sad. The exhausted storm was sighing and sobbing itself into rest. I went to the stranger’s room and listened at his door, which was slightly open. I could hear his voice, so I knocked. No one answered, but the voice kept going. I leaned in and peeped through. The man lay on his back in bed, talking in broken bursts but with energy, punctuating his words with his arms, which he thrashed about restlessly, the way sick people do in delirium.

I slipped in quietly and bent over him. His mutterings and outbursts continued. I spoke—just a single word to get his attention. In an instant his glassy eyes and ashen face lit up with pleasure, gratitude, joy, and welcome.

“Oh, Sandy, you’re here at last—how long I’ve waited for you! Sit by me—don’t leave me—never leave me again, Sandy, never again. Where is your hand?—give it to me, dear, let me hold it—there—now everything is well, everything is peaceful, and I’m happy again—_we_ are happy again, aren’t we, Sandy? You’re so dim, so vague—you’re only a mist, a cloud, but you’re _here_, and that’s enough blessedness; and I have your hand; don’t take it away—it’s only for a little while; I won’t need it for long.... Was that the child?... Hello—Central!... she doesn’t answer. Maybe she’s asleep? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and tell her good-bye.... Sandy! Yes, you’re there. I lost myself for a moment, and I thought you were gone.... How long have I been sick? It must be so; it feels like months. And those dreams—such strange and terrible dreams, Sandy! Dreams that felt as real as reality—of course delirium, but _so_ real! I thought the king was dead. I thought you were in Gaul and couldn’t get home. I thought there’d been a revolution. In the wild frenzy of those dreams, I even believed Clarence and I, with a handful of my cadets, fought and wiped out all the chivalry of England! But even that wasn’t the strangest. It felt like I was a creature from some far-off age that hadn’t even been born yet—centuries from now—and that was just as real as the rest! Yes, it seemed I’d flown back out of that age into this one, and then forward to it again, and I was left here—alone, a stranger, and forlorn—in that strange England, with an abyss of thirteen centuries yawning between me and you! between me and my home and my friends! between me and everything that’s dear to me, everything that could make life worth living!”}

As I reached my hand through the valve, holding a cup of water for the old cat, my shirt sleeves caught in the loop that held the basket in place. In an instant, it came loose from the bottom. If the whole basket had simply vanished into thin air, it could not have left my sight more suddenly or more completely. There could not have been even a tenth of a second between the basket coming free and its total disappearance, with everything inside it. My good wishes followed it down to the earth, but, of course, I had no hope that either the cat or the kittens would ever live to tell what happened.

“At six o’clock, I noticed that a large part of the visible ground to the east was covered by thick shadow. The shadow moved forward very quickly, until, five minutes before seven, everything I could see was covered by the darkness of night. Still, it was not until long after that that the rays of the setting sun stopped lighting the balloon. Even though I expected this, it still gave me a great deal of pleasure. It was clear that in the morning I would see the rising sun many hours before the people of Rotterdam, even though they were farther east. And day after day, the higher I rose, the longer I would enjoy daylight. I decided to keep a journal of my trip, counting the days from one to twenty-four hours straight, without worrying about the periods of darkness.

“At ten o’clock, feeling sleepy, I decided to lie down for the rest of the night. But a problem came up—one that seems obvious now, but that I had missed until this very moment. If I fell asleep as I planned, how would the air in the chamber be renewed while I was out? Breathing it for more than an hour, at most, would be impossible. And even if I could stretch it to an hour and a quarter, the results could be disastrous. Thinking about this made me very uneasy. After all the dangers I had already faced, it would be hard to believe that I would take this risk so seriously that I would give up on my plan and finally decide I had to come down. But my hesitation lasted only a moment.”}

And Mrs Musgrove seized the first chance of being alone with Anne to say, “Oh! Miss Anne, I can’t help but wish Mrs Charles had a bit of your way with those children. They’re quite different beings when you have them! Still, as a rule, they’re so spoiled. It’s a shame you can’t put your sister in the way of managing them. They’re as healthy and fine children as ever were seen, poor little darlings—without any partiality; but Mrs Charles doesn’t know how they ought to be treated. Bless me! they can be such a trial sometimes. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting to have them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I think Mrs Charles isn’t entirely pleased that I don’t invite them more frequently; but you know how very wrong it is to have children with someone who’s bound to be correcting them every moment—‘don’t do this,’ and ‘don’t do that;’ or else you can only keep them in tolerable order by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

She had, besides, this message from Mary. “Mrs Musgrove thinks all her servants are so steady that it would be treason to question it; but I’m sure—without any exaggeration—that her upper house-maid and laundry-maid, instead of staying at their work, are running about the village all day. I meet them wherever I go; and I declare I never enter my nursery twice without seeing something of them. If Jemima weren’t the most trustworthy, the steadiest creature in the world, it would be enough to spoil her; for she tells me they’re always coaxing her to take a walk with them.” And on Mrs Musgrove’s part, it was, “I make it a rule never to meddle in any of my daughter-in-law’s affairs, because I know it wouldn’t do; but I’ll tell you, Miss Anne, since you may be able to set things right, that I don’t have a very good opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid. I hear odd stories about her; she’s always on the go; and from what I know myself, she’s such a fine-dressed lady that she’s enough to ruin any servants she comes near. Mrs Charles swears by her, I know; but I’m only giving you this hint, so you’ll keep your eyes open; because if you notice anything wrong, you needn’t be afraid to mention it.”

Once more, Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove was too ready not to give her the precedence she deserved when they dined at the Great House with other families; and she could see no reason why she should be treated as though she were to be so much at home that she lost her place.”}]}

It then gave an altitude on my part of 132,000 feet, or five-and-twenty miles, and as a result I surveyed at that time an area of the earth’s surface amounting to no less than the three hundred-and-twentieth part of its entire superficies. At nine o’clock I had again lost sight of land to the eastward, but not before I realized that the balloon was drifting rapidly to the N. N. W. The convexity of the ocean beneath me was very clearly visible, though my view was often interrupted by masses of cloud that floated to and fro. I now observed that even the lightest vapors never rose to more than ten miles above the level of the sea.

“At half past nine I tried the experiment of throwing out a handful of feathers through the valve. They did not float as I had expected; instead, they dropped straight down, like a bullet, in a tight mass, and with the greatest velocity—out of sight in only a few seconds. At first I didn’t know what to make of this extraordinary phenomenon; I couldn’t believe that my rate of ascent had suddenly met with such a prodigious acceleration. But it soon occurred to me that the atmosphere was now far too thin to support even the feathers; that they actually fell, as they appeared to do, with great rapidity; and that I had been thrown off by the combined effect of their descent and my own rising elevation.

“By ten o’clock I found that I had very little to occupy my immediate attention. Things went smoothly, and I believed the balloon was rising with a speed increasing moment by moment, even though I no longer had any way to measure the increase. I felt no pain or uneasiness of any kind, and my spirits were better than at any point since my departure from Rotterdam. I kept myself busy, now examining the condition of my various instruments, and now regenerating the air within the chamber. I decided to handle this last task at regular intervals of forty minutes, more for the sake of preserving my health than because so frequent a renewal was absolutely necessary. In the meantime, I couldn’t help making anticipations. Fancy reveled in the wild and dreamy regions of the moon. Imagination, feeling herself for once unchained, roamed freely among the ever-changing wonders of a shadowy and unstable land. Now there were hoary, time-honored forests, craggy precipices, and waterfalls tumbling with a loud roar into bottomless abysses. Then, all at once, I found myself in still noonday solitudes, where no wind of heaven ever intruded, and where vast meadows of poppies and slender, lily-like flowers stretched out for a weary distance—silent and motionless forever.”}

It then showed an altitude for me of 132,000 feet, or five-and-twenty miles. From that point, I could survey an area of the earth’s surface that was no less than the three hundred-and-twentieth part of its total. At nine o’clock I lost sight of land again to the east, but not before I noticed that the balloon was drifting quickly toward the N. N. W. The curve of the ocean beneath me was clear to see, though my view was often broken up by clouds moving back and forth. I also noticed that even the lightest vapors never rose more than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I tried an experiment. I threw a handful of feathers out through the valve. I expected them to float, but they did not. Instead, they fell straight down, like a bullet, in a tight mass, and with the greatest speed. They were out of sight within a few seconds. At first I did not know what to think of this strange event. I could not believe that my rate of ascent had suddenly turned into such a huge acceleration. But it soon became clear to me that the air was now too thin to hold even the feathers up. They were falling, just as they looked, very fast. I had been surprised by the combined effect of their downward speed and my own rising speed.

“By ten o’clock I found that there was very little to do right then. Everything was going smoothly, and I believed the balloon was rising with a speed that kept increasing, even though I no longer had any way to measure how much it was increasing. I felt no pain and no uneasiness, and my spirits were better than at any time since I left Rotterdam. I kept busy, checking the condition of my different instruments, and then working on renewing the air inside the chamber. I decided to do this last task at regular intervals of forty minutes, more for my health than because the chamber truly needed such frequent renewal. In the meantime, I could not help imagining things. My mind wandered through the wild, dreamlike regions of the moon. My imagination, free for once, roamed wherever it pleased over the changing wonders of a shadowy, unstable land. At one moment I saw old, gray forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing loudly into bottomless pits. Then, suddenly, I was in quiet, bright midday emptiness, where no wind ever reached, and where wide meadows of poppies and thin, lily-like flowers stretched out far and far—silent and unmoving forever.”}

And Mrs Musgrove took the first chance to be alone with Anne and said, “Oh! Miss Anne, I can’t help wishing Mrs Charles had a little of your method with those children. They’re completely different with you! But then, in general, they’re so spoiled. It’s a pity you can’t put your sister in the way of managing them. They’re as fine, healthy children as ever were seen—poor little darlings—without any partiality; but Mrs Charles knows no more how they ought to be treated—! Bless me! They can be so troublesome sometimes. I assure you, Miss Anne, it stops me from wanting to see them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I believe Mrs Charles isn’t quite pleased that I don’t invite them more often; but you know it’s very bad to have children with you when you’re under an obligation to keep correcting them every moment—‘don’t do this’ and ‘don’t do that’—or else you can only keep them in tolerable order by giving them more cake than is good for them.”

She also had this message from Mary. “Mrs Musgrove thinks all her servants are so steady that it would be high treason to question it; but I’m sure—without exaggerating—that her upper house-maid and laundry-maid, instead of doing their work, are out wandering around the village all day long. I run into them wherever I go; and I declare, I never go into my nursery twice without seeing something of them. If Jemima weren’t the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to spoil her; because she tells me they’re always tempting her to go for a walk with them.” And on Mrs Musgrove’s side, it was, “I make it a rule never to interfere in any of my daughter-in-law’s concerns, because I know it wouldn’t do; but I’ll tell you, Miss Anne, because you may be able to set things right. I don’t have a very good opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid. I hear strange stories about her. She’s always out and about; and from my own knowledge, I can say she’s such a fine-dressing lady that she’s enough to ruin any servants she comes near. Mrs Charles swears by her, I know; but I’m just giving you this hint so you can keep an eye on things. If you notice anything wrong, you needn’t be afraid to mention it.”

Again, it was Mary’s complaint that Mrs Musgrove was very likely not to give her the precedence she deserved when they dined at the Great House with other families, and she saw no reason why she should be treated as if she were so much at home that she could lose her place.”}
